A library's notification board publishes updates about newly returned books to many independent display widgets. The board keeps a list of registered listeners and calls them in a loop:

```java
public void publish(BookUpdate update) {
    for (BookListener listener : listeners) {
        listener.onUpdate(update);
    }
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A configuration class in the library's catalog service is written like this and is read from every request handler:

```java
public class AppConfig {
    private static AppConfig instance;
    private AppConfig() { }
    public static AppConfig getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new AppConfig();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Which criticisms of this code are justified?

A report generator for library statistics fixes the order of the steps and lets subclasses vary two of them:

```java
public abstract class ReportGenerator {
    public final Report generate() {
        Data data = load();
        Data shaped = transform(data);
        return write(render(shaped));
    }
    protected Data load() { return Data.fromDefaultSource(); }
    protected abstract Data transform(Data data);
    protected abstract String render(Data data);
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A library's return processing system sorts returned items with interchangeable algorithms:

```java
public interface SortStrategy { void performSort(List<Item> input); }
public class BubbleSort implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class MergeSort  implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class Context { private List<Item> items; private SortStrategy sortAlgorithm; ... }
public class Policy  { private Context context; public void configure() { ... } }
```

Using the class names from this listing: the class that holds the items and a reference to the currently selected algorithm is [-spot 1]. The type that both `BubbleSort` and `MergeSort` implement is [-spot 2]. The class whose `configure()` method decides which algorithm to select is [-spot 3].

A library's notification service is being reviewed.

```java
public class NotificationService {
    private final EmailNotifier notifier = new EmailNotifier("mail.internal", 25);
    public void sendAlert(User user, String message) { /* ... */ notifier.send(user.email(), "..."); }
}
```

The reviewer's first objection is that `NotificationService` names a concrete low‑level class instead of an abstraction such as a `Notifier` interface; the principle being violated is abbreviated [-spot 1] (three letters). The reviewer's fix is to stop constructing the collaborator inside the class and supply it from outside instead — the general two‑word name for that technique is [-spot 2].
